      "title": "Phases of a Compiler",
      "teacher": "A compiler does not do its job in one giant leap. It works like an assembly line — six small stations, each doing one simple thing and handing the result to the next. This is the most important diagram in the whole unit, so let's walk the line together, station by station.",
      "explain": "A compiler translates a source program through <strong>six phases</strong>, each taking the output of the previous one. They are <strong>lexical analysis, syntax analysis, semantic analysis, intermediate code generation, code optimisation</strong> and <strong>code generation</strong>. Two components — the <strong>symbol table manager</strong> and the <strong>error handler</strong> — are not phases at all; they work <em>alongside every phase</em>.",
