      "worked": "<strong>Tracing the statement <code>position = initial + rate * 60</code> through all six phases.</strong><br>(Assume <code>position</code>, <code>initial</code> and <code>rate</code> are declared as floating-point variables.)<br><br><strong>PHASE 1 — Lexical Analysis.</strong> The characters are grouped into tokens, and the three identifiers go into the symbol table as entries 1, 2 and 3:<br><code>&lt;id, 1&gt; &lt;=&gt; &lt;id, 2&gt; &lt;+&gt; &lt;id, 3&gt; &lt;*&gt; &lt;number, 60&gt;</code><br>Spaces are discarded. There are 8 tokens.<br><br><strong>PHASE 2 — Syntax Analysis.</strong> The parser checks the tokens against the grammar and builds a syntax tree. Because <code>*</code> binds tighter than <code>+</code>, the multiplication sits deeper in the tree:<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;/&nbsp;&nbsp; \\</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;id,1&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;+</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;/&nbsp; \\</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;id,2&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;/&nbsp; \\</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;id,3&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp; 60</code><br><br><strong>PHASE 3 — Semantic Analysis.</strong> The types are checked using the symbol table. <code>rate</code> is a float but <code>60</code> is an integer, so the compiler inserts a conversion node so both operands match:<br>the leaf <code>60</code> becomes <code>inttofloat(60)</code><br>If instead <code>rate</code> had been declared as an array, this is the phase that would report the error.<br><br><strong>PHASE 4 — Intermediate Code Generation</strong> (three-address code — at most one operator per line):<br><code>t1 = inttofloat(60)</code><br><code>t2 = id3 * t1</code><br><code>t3 = id2 + t2</code><br><code>id1 = t3</code><br><br><strong>PHASE 5 — Code Optimisation.</strong> <code>inttofloat(60)</code> is a constant, so it can be worked out at compile time instead of at run time (<em>constant folding</em>), and <code>t3</code> is copied straight into <code>id1</code> so it can be eliminated (<em>copy propagation</em>). Four lines become two:<br><code>t1 = id3 * 60.0</code><br><code>id1 = id2 + t1</code><br><br><strong>PHASE 6 — Code Generation.</strong> Registers are allocated and target assembly is emitted (F = floating-point, # = a literal value):<br><code>LDF&nbsp;&nbsp; R2, id3</code><br><code>MULF&nbsp; R2, R2, #60.0</code><br><code>LDF&nbsp;&nbsp; R1, id2</code><br><code>ADDF&nbsp; R1, R1, R2</code><br><code>STF&nbsp;&nbsp; id1, R1</code><br><br><strong>Running throughout:</strong> the <strong>symbol table</strong> held the entries for <code>position</code>, <code>initial</code> and <code>rate</code> with their types — phase 1 created them and phase 3 read them back. The <strong>error handler</strong> stood ready at every phase.",
