      "examAnswer": "A compiler translates a source program into a target program through a sequence of six phases. Lexical analysis reads the source characters and groups them into tokens, discarding whitespace and comments. Syntax analysis takes the token stream and verifies that it conforms to the grammar of the language, producing a parse tree. Semantic analysis checks the meaning of the program using the symbol table, verifying declarations and type compatibility and inserting any required type conversions. Intermediate code generation produces a machine-independent representation, commonly three-address code. Code optimisation, which is an optional phase, transforms this intermediate code so that it executes more efficiently or occupies less space. Finally, code generation produces the target machine or assembly code and performs register allocation. In addition, the symbol table manager and the error handler are not phases but supporting components that interact with every phase of compilation.",
