      "examAnswer": "The six phases of a compiler are conventionally grouped into two parts, namely the analysis phase and the synthesis phase, also called the front end and the back end. The analysis phase comprises lexical analysis, syntax analysis, semantic analysis and intermediate code generation; it decomposes the source program, determines its meaning, records information in the symbol table and produces an intermediate representation. The synthesis phase comprises code optimisation and code generation, and constructs the target program from the intermediate representation. The front end depends upon the source language but is independent of the target machine, while the back end depends upon the target machine but is independent of the source language. This separation is of great practical value, since supporting M source languages on N target machines requires only M front ends and N back ends rather than M multiplied by N complete compilers. A phase should be distinguished from a pass, a pass being one complete reading of the source program, during which several phases may be carried out.",
