      "worked": "<strong>Bootstrapping a compiler for a new language 'L' on a machine that already has a C compiler.</strong><br><br><strong>Step 1.</strong> Pick a small subset of L — call it S. S has only the essentials: variables, assignment, <code>if</code> and <code>while</code>. No fancy features.<br><br><strong>Step 2.</strong> Write a compiler for S <em>in C</em>. Call it <code>C1</code>.<br>Compile <code>C1</code> with the existing C compiler → you now have a <strong>working, running compiler for S</strong>.<br><br><strong>Step 3.</strong> Now write a compiler for the <strong>full</strong> language L — but write it using only the features of S. Call this source <code>C2</code>.<br><br><strong>Step 4.</strong> Feed <code>C2</code> into <code>C1</code>. Since <code>C2</code> is written only in S, and <code>C1</code> understands S, this works. The output is a <strong>running compiler for the full language L</strong>.<br><br><strong>Step 5 (the elegant part).</strong> Now rewrite the compiler using the full power of L, and compile <em>that</em> with the compiler from Step 4. From here on, L compiles itself and C is never needed again. The language has become <strong>self-hosting</strong>.<br><br><strong>And now porting, using the same compiler.</strong><br>Suppose the compiler currently produces code for an Intel processor and you want it to produce code for an ARM processor.<br>Keep: the <strong>entire front end</strong> — lexical, syntax, semantic analysis and intermediate code generation. Nothing about the language has changed.<br>Rewrite: the <strong>back end</strong> only — code generation for ARM instructions and ARM registers.<br>Run the new compiler <em>on the Intel machine</em> to produce ARM code — at this moment it is acting as a <strong>cross compiler</strong>. Then use it to compile itself, producing an ARM-native compiler that runs on the ARM machine. The port is complete.",
      "mistake": "A cross compiler is defined by <em>where its output runs</em>, not by what language it accepts. A compiler on your laptop that produces code for an Android phone is a cross compiler even though it is an ordinary C compiler in every other respect.",
      "examAnswer": "Compilers may be classified as single-pass or multi-pass according to the number of times the source program is read, as optimising compilers when they perform extensive improvement of the target code, as incremental compilers when they recompile only modified portions, and as just-in-time compilers when translation occurs during execution. A cross compiler is a compiler that executes on one machine but generates object code for a different machine, and is essential for developing software for embedded systems and for newly designed processors. Bootstrapping is the technique of writing a compiler for a language in that same language: a compiler for a small subset of the language is first written in an existing language, and this is then used to compile a fuller compiler written in that subset, until the language is able to compile itself and becomes self-hosting. Porting, or retargeting, is the process of adapting an existing compiler to generate code for a new machine, which is accomplished by rewriting only the back end while retaining the machine-independent front end.",
      "remember": "Bootstrapping = the language compiles itself, starting from a small subset. Porting = keep the front end, rewrite the back end. Cross compiler = built here, runs there.",
      "why": "Every Android app on your phone was built by a cross compiler running on a laptop. And every modern language — C, Go, Rust — is self-hosted through bootstrapping.",
