      "title": "Assembly Language: Elements and the Assembler's Job",
      "teacher": "Machine code is pure numbers — <code>00010100</code> means 'add'. Nobody can write software like that for long. So somebody had a simple, brilliant idea: give every instruction a short name a human can remember, and let a program do the number-lookup. That program is the assembler, and those short names are mnemonics.",
      "explain": "<strong>Assembly language</strong> is a low-level language in which each machine instruction is written as a short symbolic name called a <strong>mnemonic</strong>, with symbolic names for memory locations instead of raw addresses. An <strong>assembler</strong> translates it into machine code. The mapping is essentially <strong>one statement to one machine instruction</strong>.",
