        "<strong>Label</strong> — a symbolic name for the address of that statement, so you can refer to it without knowing the number.",
        "<strong>Operands</strong> — what the instruction acts on: a register (<code>AREG</code>), a symbol (<code>ALPHA</code>), or a literal (<code>='5'</code>).",
        "Three kinds of statement: <strong>imperative</strong> (a real executable instruction), <strong>declarative</strong> (reserve or initialise storage — <code>DS</code>, <code>DC</code>), and <strong>assembler directives</strong>.",
        "<strong>Directives (pseudo-ops)</strong> instruct the assembler itself and generate no machine code: <code>START</code>, <code>END</code>, <code>ORIGIN</code>, <code>EQU</code>, <code>LTORG</code>.",
        "The assembler maintains a <strong>Location Counter (LC)</strong> which tracks the address of the statement being processed.",
        "Assembler tables: <strong>Mnemonic Table (MOT)</strong>, <strong>Symbol Table (ST)</strong>, <strong>Literal Table (LT)</strong> and <strong>Pool Table</strong>.",
        "Assembly is <strong>machine dependent</strong> — assembly written for an Intel CPU will not run on an ARM CPU."
