      "realLife": "Mnemonics are exactly like saved contacts on your phone. The phone really dials 9425XXXXXX, but you tap 'Mummy'. You remember the name; the phone does the number lookup. The assembler is that contact list for machine instructions.",
      "worked": "<strong>A small assembly program, fully labelled.</strong><br><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;START&nbsp; 100&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;; directive - begin at address 100</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;MOVER&nbsp; AREG, ALPHA&nbsp;; imperative - load ALPHA into register A</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ADD&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; AREG, ONE&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;; imperative - add ONE to it</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;MOVEM&nbsp; AREG, BETA&nbsp;&nbsp;; imperative - store the result in BETA</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;STOP&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;; imperative - halt</code><br><code>ALPHA&nbsp;&nbsp; DC&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'5'&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;; declarative - one word holding 5</code><br><code>BETA&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; DS&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;; declarative - reserve one empty word</code><br><code>ONE&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; DC&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'1'&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;; declarative - one word holding 1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;END&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;; directive - end of source</code><br><br><strong>Picking the program apart:</strong><br><strong>Labels:</strong> ALPHA, BETA, ONE &nbsp;(names for addresses)<br><strong>Mnemonics:</strong> MOVER, ADD, MOVEM, STOP &nbsp;(operations)<br><strong>Operands:</strong> AREG is a register; ALPHA, ONE, BETA are symbols<br><strong>Directives:</strong> START and END &nbsp;(no machine code produced)<br><strong>Declaratives:</strong> DC and DS &nbsp;(reserve memory, produce no executable instruction)<br><strong>Comments:</strong> everything after each semicolon — ignored completely<br><br><strong>Notice the problem hiding in line 2.</strong> The instruction <code>MOVER AREG, ALPHA</code> needs the address of ALPHA. But ALPHA is not defined until line 6, much further down. When the assembler reaches line 2 on a first reading, it simply does not know the answer yet. This is called a <strong>forward reference</strong>, and it is exactly why assemblers need two passes.",
      "mistake": "Students often say that <code>START</code> and <code>END</code> are instructions. They are <strong>directives</strong> — they command the assembler at assembly time and produce no machine code at all. Likewise <code>DS</code> and <code>DC</code> are declaratives: they reserve memory but are not executed.",
      "examAnswer": "Assembly language is a low-level programming language in which machine instructions are represented by symbolic mnemonic operation codes and memory locations are referred to by symbolic names, so that there is essentially a one-to-one correspondence between assembly statements and machine instructions. An assembly language statement consists of four fields, namely an optional label, a mnemonic operation code, one or more operands and an optional comment. Assembly statements are of three kinds: imperative statements, which correspond to executable machine instructions; declarative statements such as DS and DC, which reserve or initialise storage; and assembler directives such as START, END, ORIGIN and EQU, which direct the assembler itself and generate no machine code. An assembler translates such a program into machine code, maintaining a location counter together with a mnemonic table, a symbol table and a literal table. Assembly language is machine dependent, since its mnemonics correspond directly to the instruction set of a particular processor.",
      "remember": "Label, Mnemonic, Operand, Comment. Directives command the ASSEMBLER; imperatives command the MACHINE.",
