    {
      "title": "Assembler Design: Single-Pass and Two-Pass",
      "teacher": "Here is the puzzle from the last topic. Line 2 of your program says 'load ALPHA' — but ALPHA is not defined until line 6. The assembler has not read that far yet. So what address should it write? This one small problem is the reason assemblers are designed the way they are.",
      "explain": "A <strong>forward reference</strong> is a use of a symbol before it has been defined. A <strong>two-pass assembler</strong> solves it by reading the source twice: <strong>Pass 1</strong> defines all symbols and builds the symbol table, and <strong>Pass 2</strong> generates the object code with every address now known. A <strong>single-pass assembler</strong> reads the source once and fixes forward references afterwards by a technique called <strong>backpatching</strong>.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Forward reference</strong> = a symbol is used before it is defined. This is the central problem of assembler design.",
        "<strong>Pass 1 jobs:</strong> process the location counter, enter every label and its address in the <strong>symbol table</strong>, build the literal table, process directives.",
        "<strong>Pass 1 does NOT generate object code</strong> — it only collects information.",
        "<strong>Pass 2 jobs:</strong> read the source again, look up each symbol's address in the symbol table, convert mnemonics to opcodes, and <strong>generate the object code</strong>.",
        "By the time Pass 2 starts, <strong>every</strong> symbol is already known, so a forward reference is no longer a problem.",
        "<strong>Single-pass assembler</strong> — generates code immediately, leaves forward-referenced addresses blank, and records them in a <strong>Table of Incomplete Instructions (TII)</strong>.",
        "When the symbol is finally defined, the single-pass assembler goes back and fills in the blanks — this is <strong>backpatching</strong>.",
        "Single pass is <strong>faster</strong> and reads the source only once; two pass is <strong>simpler, cleaner and more flexible</strong>.",
        "Most real assemblers are <strong>two-pass</strong>, because the design is far easier to get right and to extend."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "The Location Counter — the heart of Pass 1",
          "text": "The LC is set by the <code>START</code> directive and holds the address that the current statement will occupy. After processing each statement, the assembler advances the LC by the number of words that statement will take. Whenever a label appears, the current value of the LC is exactly that label's address, and the pair (label, LC value) goes straight into the symbol table."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why Pass 2 is the easy pass",
          "text": "Pass 2 does no discovery at all. Every symbol already has an address, every literal already has a place. Pass 2 simply walks the source again, replacing each mnemonic with a number from the mnemonic table and each symbol with a number from the symbol table, and writes out the object program. All the thinking was done in Pass 1."
        },
        {
          "title": "Backpatching in a single-pass assembler",
          "text": "When a single-pass assembler meets an undefined symbol, it writes the instruction with a blank address field and adds an entry to the Table of Incomplete Instructions saying 'the address of symbol X is still needed at location 100'. Later, when X is defined, the assembler returns to location 100 and patches the correct address in. It works, but the bookkeeping is fiddly and error-prone."
        },
        {
          "title": "Choosing between the two",
          "text": "Single pass is preferred when speed matters and the language is simple — for instance, a small loader or an assembler embedded in a system with tight time limits. Two pass is preferred almost everywhere else, because separating 'find out the addresses' from 'write the code' produces a design that is much easier to understand, test and extend."
        }
