      "realLife": "Imagine addressing wedding invitation envelopes while the guest list is still being finalised. Two-pass: wait until the full list is ready, then write every envelope once, correctly. Single-pass: start writing immediately, leave the unknown addresses blank, and go back to fill them in later. Both work — but one causes far fewer mistakes.",
      "worked": "<strong>Two-pass assembly of the program from the previous topic.</strong><br><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;START&nbsp; 100</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;MOVER&nbsp; AREG, ALPHA</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ADD&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; AREG, ONE</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;MOVEM&nbsp; AREG, BETA</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;STOP</code><br><code>ALPHA&nbsp;&nbsp; DC&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'5'</code><br><code>BETA&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; DS&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1</code><br><code>ONE&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; DC&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'1'</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;END</code><br><br><strong>PASS 1 — walk through with the Location Counter.</strong> <code>START 100</code> sets LC = 100. Each statement below occupies one word, so the LC advances by 1 each time.<br><code>LC=100&nbsp; MOVER AREG, ALPHA&nbsp;&nbsp; (ALPHA unknown - never mind, Pass 1 does not generate code)</code><br><code>LC=101&nbsp; ADD&nbsp;&nbsp; AREG, ONE</code><br><code>LC=102&nbsp; MOVEM AREG, BETA</code><br><code>LC=103&nbsp; STOP</code><br><code>LC=104&nbsp; ALPHA DC '5'&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(label ALPHA seen - record address 104)</code><br><code>LC=105&nbsp; BETA&nbsp; DS 1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(label BETA&nbsp; seen - record address 105)</code><br><code>LC=106&nbsp; ONE&nbsp;&nbsp; DC '1'&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(label ONE&nbsp;&nbsp; seen - record address 106)</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; END</code><br><br><strong>Symbol table at the end of Pass 1:</strong><br><code>SYMBOL&nbsp; | ADDRESS</code><br><code>--------+---------</code><br><code>ALPHA&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 104</code><br><code>BETA&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 105</code><br><code>ONE&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 106</code><br><br><strong>PASS 2 — now generate the object code.</strong> Read the source a second time. This time <em>every</em> symbol has a known address, so the forward reference to ALPHA on the very first instruction is no longer a problem — the table already says 104.<br><code>Address | Instruction&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; | Operand address</code><br><code>--------+--------------------+----------------</code><br><code>&nbsp; 100&nbsp;&nbsp; | MOVER AREG&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 104&nbsp; (ALPHA)</code><br><code>&nbsp; 101&nbsp;&nbsp; | ADD&nbsp;&nbsp; AREG&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 106&nbsp; (ONE)</code><br><code>&nbsp; 102&nbsp;&nbsp; | MOVEM AREG&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp; 105&nbsp; (BETA)</code><br><code>&nbsp; 103&nbsp;&nbsp; | STOP&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; -</code><br><code>&nbsp; 104&nbsp;&nbsp; | (constant 5)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; -</code><br><code>&nbsp; 105&nbsp;&nbsp; | (reserved, empty)&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; -</code><br><code>&nbsp; 106&nbsp;&nbsp; | (constant 1)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; |&nbsp;&nbsp; -</code><br><br><strong>The same program in a SINGLE-PASS assembler.</strong><br>At LC=100 the assembler meets ALPHA, which is undefined. It writes the instruction with a <strong>blank</strong> operand field and records in the Table of Incomplete Instructions: <code>(100, ALPHA)</code>. It does the same at 101 for ONE and at 102 for BETA.<br>When it later reaches <code>ALPHA DC '5'</code> at LC=104, it looks in the TII, finds the pending entry for location 100, and <strong>backpatches</strong> 104 into it. Same for ONE and BETA.<br>Final result: identical object code, reached by a more complicated route.",
      "mistake": "Pass 1 does <strong>not</strong> produce any object code. Its only job is to work out addresses and build the tables. Writing 'Pass 1 generates machine code' in the exam loses the marks for the whole question, because it shows the purpose of the two-pass design was not understood.",
      "examAnswer": "A forward reference occurs when a symbol is used in an assembly language program before it has been defined, so that its address is not yet known when the statement is first encountered. A two-pass assembler resolves this by reading the source program twice. During the first pass, the assembler processes the location counter, records every label together with its address in the symbol table, constructs the literal table and processes assembler directives, but it generates no object code. During the second pass, the assembler reads the source again, replaces each mnemonic with its machine operation code and each symbol with the address obtained from the symbol table, and produces the object program; since all symbols are defined by this stage, forward references present no difficulty. A single-pass assembler instead generates code immediately, leaving the operand field of a forward-referencing instruction blank and recording it in a table of incomplete instructions, and fills in the correct addresses later by a process known as backpatching. The two-pass design is more widely used because it is simpler and more easily extended, whereas the single-pass design is faster but more complex to implement.",
      "remember": "Pass 1 = find the ADDRESSES (build the symbol table, no code). Pass 2 = write the CODE. Two passes exist because of forward references.",
      "why": "This is a guaranteed exam question, and the symbol table idea reappears everywhere — compilers, linkers and debuggers all keep one for exactly the same reason.",
      "practice": "Take the program in the worked example, change START to 200, and rebuild the symbol table and the Pass 2 object code table. Then explain in three lines how a single-pass assembler would handle the reference to ALPHA."
