          "text": "A <strong>macro</strong> is expanded at assembly time, so the code is physically copied in at every call site. There is no jump, no return, no stack work — but the program grows with every use. A <strong>function</strong> exists once in memory; each call jumps to it and returns. That keeps the program small but costs time on every call. So macros trade <em>space</em> for <em>speed</em>, and functions trade <em>speed</em> for <em>space</em>."
