      "worked": "<strong>Defining and expanding a macro.</strong><br><br><strong>Step 1 — the macro definition.</strong> Note the formal parameter <code>&amp;ARG</code>:<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MACRO</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; INCR&nbsp;&nbsp; &amp;ARG</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MOVER&nbsp; AREG, &amp;ARG</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; ADD&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; AREG, ONE</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MOVEM&nbsp; AREG, &amp;ARG</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MEND</code><br>This block generates <strong>no code at all</strong> by itself. It is only stored away, in the MNT and MDT.<br><br><strong>Step 2 — the program, using two macro calls:</strong><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; START&nbsp; 100</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; INCR&nbsp;&nbsp; DATA1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; INCR&nbsp;&nbsp; DATA2</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; STOP</code><br>Only 4 lines written by the programmer.<br><br><strong>Step 3 — after macro expansion</strong> (this is what the assembler actually receives). Each call is replaced by a full copy of the body, with <code>&amp;ARG</code> substituted:<br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; START&nbsp; 100</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MOVER&nbsp; AREG, DATA1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; ADD&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; AREG, ONE</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MOVEM&nbsp; AREG, DATA1</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MOVER&nbsp; AREG, DATA2</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; ADD&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; AREG, ONE</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MOVEM&nbsp; AREG, DATA2</code><br><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; STOP</code><br><br><strong>What to notice.</strong> The programmer wrote 4 lines; the assembler received 8. The three-instruction body appears <strong>twice</strong> — once for each call. There is no jump and no return anywhere, so it runs at full speed, but the code is duplicated. Call the macro fifty times and those three instructions appear fifty times. That is the space-for-speed trade, made visible.",
      "mistake": "A macro is not a function. It does <strong>not</strong> jump anywhere and it does <strong>not</strong> return. Its body is physically copied into the program at every call, which is why heavy macro use makes the executable bigger. Also, macro expansion happens before assembly, not during execution.",
      "examAnswer": "A macro is a named sequence of instructions which is defined once and may then be used repeatedly simply by writing its name. A macro definition is enclosed between the MACRO and MEND directives and may include formal parameters, while a macro call supplies the corresponding actual parameters. A macro processor is a system program that performs four functions: recognising macro definitions, saving them in the macro name table and macro definition table, recognising macro calls, and expanding those calls by substituting the actual parameters for the formal parameters in a copy of the macro body. Macro expansion takes place before assembly or compilation, so the translator receives a program containing no macros. A macro differs from a function in that the macro body is physically copied into the program at each point of call, which avoids the overhead of a jump and return and therefore executes faster, but increases the size of the program, whereas a function exists as a single copy in memory and is invoked at run time.",
      "remember": "Macro = COPY-PASTE at assembly time. Function = JUMP-AND-RETURN at run time. Macro saves time, function saves space.",
      "why": "The same idea is the <code>#define</code> you already use in C. Understanding that a macro is textual substitution explains a whole family of confusing C bugs.",
      "practice": "Write a macro called ADDTWO that adds two symbolic values and stores the result in a third. Show the macro definition, two calls with different arguments, and the fully expanded code. Then list four differences between a macro and a function."
    },
    {
      "title": "Linkers and Loaders",
      "teacher": "Your program is compiled — so why can't it run yet? Because real programs are written in pieces, and those pieces have never met each other. Someone has to introduce them, and then someone has to carry the finished thing into memory. Those two someones are the linker and the loader.",
      "explain": "A <strong>linker</strong> combines several object modules and library routines into one executable program, resolving every <strong>external reference</strong> — a name used in one module but defined in another. A <strong>loader</strong> then brings that executable into main memory, adjusts addresses to where it actually landed, and starts it running.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Object module</strong> = the output of one compilation. It is not yet runnable on its own.",
        "<strong>External reference</strong> — a symbol used in one module but defined in another (or in a library).",
        "<strong>Linker jobs:</strong> combine the modules, <strong>resolve external references</strong> (symbol resolution), and adjust addresses (<strong>relocation</strong>).",
        "Linker <strong>input</strong> = object modules + libraries. Linker <strong>output</strong> = one executable file.",
        "<strong>Static linking</strong> — library code is copied into the executable. Bigger file, but self-contained.",
        "<strong>Dynamic linking</strong> — the library is linked at load or run time (a <code>.dll</code> or <code>.so</code>). Smaller files, shared by many programs.",
        "<strong>Loader jobs:</strong> <strong>allocation</strong> (reserve memory), <strong>linking</strong>, <strong>relocation</strong> (fix addresses for the real load address) and <strong>loading</strong> (copy in and start).",
        "Types of loader: <strong>absolute, bootstrap, relocating</strong> and <strong>direct-linking</strong>.",
        "Order of events: <strong>Compiler/Assembler → Linker → Loader → running program</strong>."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "What symbol resolution really means",
          "text": "Suppose <code>main.c</code> calls <code>printf</code>. The compiler produces an object file in which the call has an empty address and a note saying 'I need a symbol called printf'. The linker searches the other object modules and the standard library, finds the definition, and writes its address into the call. If it cannot find it, you get the familiar error 'undefined reference to printf' — and that message comes from the <strong>linker</strong>, not the compiler."
        },
        {
          "title": "Relocation — why addresses have to be adjusted",
          "text": "Each module is compiled as though it started at address 0. When the linker joins four such modules, only one can actually begin at 0; the others must be shifted, and every address inside them adjusted by the same amount. The loader may shift the whole program again, depending on where free memory happens to be. That adjustment is relocation."
        },
        {
          "title": "The four functions of a loader",
          "text": "<strong>Allocation</strong> — reserve enough memory for the program. <strong>Linking</strong> — resolve any remaining symbol references, particularly for dynamic libraries. <strong>Relocation</strong> — adjust addresses to match the actual load location. <strong>Loading</strong> — physically copy the program into memory and transfer control to its first instruction. A common mnemonic is <strong>A-L-R-L</strong>."
        },
        {
          "title": "Types of loader",
          "text": "An <strong>absolute loader</strong> simply places the program at the fixed address it was built for, doing no relocation — simple but inflexible. A <strong>relocating loader</strong> can place the program anywhere and adjust addresses accordingly. A <strong>direct-linking loader</strong> performs linking and loading together. A <strong>bootstrap loader</strong> is the tiny program in ROM that loads the operating system when the machine is switched on."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "Think of a project report written by four classmates in four separate files. The <strong>linker</strong> is whoever merges the four files, fixes all the cross-references ('as described in Section 3') and renumbers every page. The <strong>loader</strong> is whoever then opens the finished report on the projector so everyone can actually read it.",
      "table": {
        "headers": ["", "Linker", "Loader"],
        "rows": [
          ["Input", "Object modules and libraries", "The executable file"],
          ["Output", "A single executable file", "The program running in memory"],
          ["Main job", "Resolve external references, combine modules", "Allocate memory, relocate, load and start"],
          ["When it runs", "After compiling/assembling, before execution", "At the moment the program is executed"],
          ["Runs how often", "Once, at build time", "Every time the program is started"],
          ["Typical error", "'undefined reference to ...'", "'not enough memory to load program'"]
