      "worked": "<strong>Following a program from source code to execution.</strong><br><br>A program is written in two files:<br><code>main.c&nbsp;&nbsp; -&gt; contains main(), and CALLS a function total()</code><br><code>calc.c&nbsp;&nbsp; -&gt; DEFINES the function total()</code><br><br><strong>Step 1 — Compile each file separately.</strong><br><code>main.c&nbsp; --compiler--&gt;&nbsp; main.o</code><br><code>calc.c&nbsp; --compiler--&gt;&nbsp; calc.o</code><br>Inside <code>main.o</code> the call to total() has <strong>no address</strong> — just a note: 'external reference: total, needed at offset 24'. Neither <code>.o</code> file can run on its own.<br><br><strong>Step 2 — The linker combines them.</strong><br><code>main.o + calc.o + standard library&nbsp; --linker--&gt;&nbsp; program.exe</code><br>The linker: (a) places main.o at offset 0 and calc.o after it, (b) looks up 'total' and finds it defined in calc.o, (c) writes that address into the pending call in main.o, and (d) relocates every address in calc.o by the amount it was shifted.<br>If 'total' had been spelled wrongly, the linker would stop here with <em>undefined reference to total</em>.<br><br><strong>Step 3 — The loader runs it.</strong> You double-click <code>program.exe</code>.<br><code>ALLOCATION&nbsp;- the loader asks the OS for, say, 8 KB of memory; the OS gives address 5000</code><br><code>LINKING&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; - any dynamic library references are resolved now</code><br><code>RELOCATION - the program was built for address 0, so 5000 is added to every address</code><br><code>LOADING&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; - the code is copied into memory and control jumps to the first instruction</code><br><br><strong>The whole chain:</strong><br><code>source .c -&gt; [COMPILER] -&gt; .o objects -&gt; [LINKER] -&gt; .exe -&gt; [LOADER] -&gt; running in memory</code><br><br><strong>The one-line test to tell them apart:</strong> the <strong>linker</strong> runs once when you build the program; the <strong>loader</strong> runs every single time you start it.",
      "mistake": "The linker does <strong>not</strong> put the program into memory, and the loader does <strong>not</strong> combine object files. Students frequently swap the two. Remember: linker = build time, once; loader = run time, every time you open the program.",
      "examAnswer": "A linker is a system program that accepts one or more object modules produced by a compiler or assembler, together with any required library routines, and combines them into a single executable program. Its principal functions are symbol resolution, in which external references, that is symbols used in one module but defined in another, are matched with their definitions, and relocation, in which the addresses within each module are adjusted to account for its position in the combined program. Linking may be static, in which library code is incorporated into the executable file, or dynamic, in which libraries are linked at load time or run time and may be shared between programs. A loader is a system program that brings the executable into main memory and prepares it for execution, performing four functions: allocation of memory, linking of any remaining references, relocation of addresses to the actual load address, and loading of the program followed by the transfer of control to it. Loaders may be absolute, relocating, direct-linking or bootstrap loaders. The linker executes once when the program is built, whereas the loader executes each time the program is run.",
      "remember": "Linker JOINS the pieces (once, at build time). Loader LIFTS it into memory (every time you run it). Loader jobs: Allocate, Link, Relocate, Load.",
      "why": "'Undefined reference' is the single most common error a C student hits, and knowing it comes from the linker — not the compiler — tells you instantly to look for a missing file or library, not a syntax error.",
